NAME
    cov - record which code actually ran

SYNOPSIS
    cov on|off
    cov clear
    cov <addr> [<len>]
    cov mark
    cov new <addr> [<len>]
    cov funcs [<count>]
    cov save|load <file>
    cov <cpu> on|off|clear|<addr> [<len>]

DESCRIPTION
    Records every 24-bit address executed between 'cov on' and 'cov off',
    then answers 'did control flow ever reach here' for any range. A
    breakpoint can only say whether execution is at an address right now,
    so a breakpoint that never fires proves nothing on its own; this says
    outright that a routine never ran, which is the answer that retires a
    suspect. Pair it with 'callers': walk up from a routine that never ran
    until you reach a caller that did, and the branch between the two is
    the one that skipped it.

    Reports executed addresses, not instruction boundaries you guessed at -
    so it also settles where 'disasm' has mis-sized an immediate, since the
    recorded addresses are the real opcode boundaries.

    Recording is off by default and costs one bool test while disarmed;
    armed, it allocates a 2MB bitmap per processor recorded.

DIFFERENTIAL COVERAGE
    'cov mark' freezes what has run so far; 'cov new <addr>' then reports
    only what has run SINCE. This is the sharpest tool here, because it
    converts a question about code into a question about the machine: mark,
    press the button (open the door, take the damage, trigger the glitch),
    and every address 'cov new' reports is code that ran because of what
    you just did. Whole-run coverage cannot separate that from the boot
    sequence and the per-frame loop, which together dwarf it.

    Marking does not stop or clear recording, so several marks in a session
    just move the baseline forward. 'cov clear' drops the mark along with
    everything else. A mark costs a second 2MB bitmap.

DISCOVERED ROUTINES
    'cov funcs' lists every address a call actually landed on while
    recording, with how many times each was entered and whether it was
    reached by a call or by an interrupt vector. These are real entry
    points, observed rather than inferred - a static scan finds routines
    reached by a literal JSR, and misses every one reached through a jump
    table or a computed pointer, which on a SNES game is most of them.

    Output is designed to be pasted into 'label': the addresses are exactly
    the ones worth naming, and naming them makes every later 'bt', 'bp
    list' and 'profile' readable. Where 'profile' ranks routines by the
    time they cost, this enumerates them, including the cheap ones.

PERSISTENCE
    'cov save <file>' writes the bitmap and the discovered routines to
    Logs/<CoreName>/<file>; 'cov load' MERGES a saved map back in rather
    than replacing what is recorded, so maps from several sessions
    accumulate into one. A map of a whole playthrough is worth far more
    than a map of one sitting, and no single sitting reaches every
    routine.

    Name it with a .covmap extension - the repo gitignores that, and a map
    is a recording of one person's playthrough rather than source.

    The file is a fixed header, the routine table, then the raw bitmap.
    It is keyed to nothing - loading a map taken from a different ROM will
    quietly merge nonsense, so keep the filename tied to the game.

    The name is resolved as 'dump' and 'load' resolve theirs: from
    Logs/<CoreName>/, or from the root with a leading '/', and refused if
    it lands above the root - see 'man dump'.

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word records another chip's instruction stream instead - see
    'man cpus' for the names. Each chip gets its own bitmap, because each
    runs its own code: an address covered on the SA-1 says nothing about
    whether the main CPU ever executed the same number. 'cop' still works
    as an alias for whichever cartridge coprocessor is present.

EXAMPLES
    cov on
    cov 10F452 10
    cov mark
    cov new 108000 8000
    cov funcs 20
    cov save alttp.covmap
    cov gsu 0A80E9 40
    cov spc on

SEE ALSO
